the cause of feeling worried: The news of a damaged bridge on the route was ____ for the drivers.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"worry"},{"id":"B","text":"worries"},{"id":"C","text":"worrying"},{"id":"D","text":"worried"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The -ing form describes what produces the feeling. A person can also cause a feeling, so do not use a people-versus-things shortcut. Focus on **cause of feeling** in this question.","explanation":"Use worrying for the situation that causes the drivers to feel worried.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"cause-of-feeling","contextKey":"emotion-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-ed-ing-adjectives"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"c1458258555bbccb39cc21bef13eae40da8f4dd38ec325d1688a26a1f2848349","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **cause of feeling**. The -ing form describes what produces the feeling. A person can also cause a feeling, so do not use a people-versus-things shortcut.","Use worrying for the situation that causes the drivers to feel worried.","Choose **worrying**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is not the adjective form for the role asked about: the cause of the feeling. Use worrying for the situation that causes the drivers to feel worried.","B":"This is not the adjective form for the role asked about: the cause of the feeling. Use worrying for the situation that causes the drivers to feel worried.","C":"Use worrying for the situation that causes the drivers to feel worried.","D":"This is not the adjective form for the role asked about: the cause of the feeling. Use worrying for the situation that causes the drivers to feel worried."}}},{"id":"022ff0d2-1fcb-539b-a7e5-4caa3669be7e","topicSlug":"ed-ing-adjectives","level":"A2","prompt":"Choose the adjective pair from the “worry” family: The news of a damaged bridge on the route was ____; the drivers felt ____.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"worried / worried"},{"id":"B","text":"worrying / worrying"},{"id":"C","text":"worrying / worried"},{"id":"D","text":"worried / worrying"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Read who experiences the emotion and what causes it. The two forms belong to the same adjective family but have different roles. Focus on **paired forms** in this question.","explanation":"The cause takes worrying; the experiencers feel worried.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"paired-forms","contextKey":"emotion-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-ed-ing-adjectives"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"67e854ed155c980a91b14deb36c8a9aa3cd212fe669533825b20d5de75da23d3","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **paired forms**. Read who experiences the emotion and what causes it. The two forms belong to the same adjective family but have different roles.","The cause takes worrying; the experiencers feel worried.","Choose **worrying / worried**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"At least one adjective in this pair assigns the wrong role: **-ed** normally describes the experiencer and **-ing** the cause. The cause takes worrying; the experiencers feel worried.","B":"At least one adjective in this pair assigns the wrong role: **-ed** normally describes the experiencer and **-ing** the cause. The cause takes worrying; the experiencers feel worried.","C":"The cause takes worrying; the experiencers feel worried.","D":"At least one adjective in this pair assigns the wrong role: **-ed** normally describes the experiencer and **-ing** the cause. The cause takes worrying; the experiencers feel worried."}}},{"id":"6cc447f4-3b12-592b-a1a8-7a7759ceaba2","topicSlug":"ed-ing-adjectives","level":"A2","prompt":"“The drivers found the news of a damaged bridge on the route worrying.” What does worrying tell us?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The situation caused the drivers to feel worried"},{"id":"B","text":"The situation itself felt worried"},{"id":"C","text":"The people caused the situation to feel worried"},{"id":"D","text":"The sentence states when the situation happened"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Read who experiences the emotion and what causes it. The two forms belong to the same adjective family but have different roles. Focus on **meaning** in this question.","explanation":"worrying describes the effect of the situation on the drivers. It is not the experiencer''s -ed adjective.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"meaning","contextKey":"emotion-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-ed-ing-adjectives"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"de02b33df23947b189295c365f400d926889d421e46bed328e86554879e5a26e","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **meaning**. Read who experiences the emotion and what causes it. The two forms belong to the same adjective family but have different roles.","worrying describes the effect of the situation on the drivers. It is not the experiencer''s -ed adjective.","Choose **The situation caused the drivers to feel worried**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"worrying describes the effect of the situation on the drivers. It is not the experiencer''s -ed adjective.","B":"This interpretation reverses or loses the distinction between **the person feeling the emotion** and **the cause of the emotion**. worrying describes the effect of the situation on the drivers. It is not the experiencer''s -ed adjective.","C":"This interpretation reverses or loses the distinction between **the person feeling the emotion** and **the cause of the emotion**. worrying describes the effect of the situation on the drivers. It is not the experiencer''s -ed adjective.","D":"This interpretation reverses or loses the distinction between **the person feeling the emotion** and **the cause of the emotion**. worrying describes the effect of the situation on the drivers. It is not the experiencer''s -ed adjective."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Many paired adjectives separate an experience from its cause. An -ed adjective commonly describes how someone feels, while the related -ing adjective describes what produces that feeling. The distinction is about meaning, not a rule that people always take -ed and things always take -ing."],"rules":[{"title":"The experienced feeling","explanation":"In these adjective pairs, the -ed form describes how someone feels. Context identifies the experiencer.","examples":["The climbers felt exhausted.","The children seemed delighted.","The speaker was **boring**, so the audience felt **bored**."],"pattern":"I am **bored**. / The lesson is **boring**.","paragraphs":["Ask who experiences the emotion. That experiencer commonly takes the -ed adjective: the confused reader, the interested visitor. The adjective can follow be or come before a noun. It describes a state; it is not automatically a passive sentence just because it ends in -ed."]},{"title":"The cause of the feeling","explanation":"The -ing form describes what produces the feeling. A person can also cause a feeling, so do not use a people-versus-things shortcut.","examples":["The climb was exhausting.","An entertaining storyteller can hold our attention.","I was **surprised** by the **surprising** result."],"pattern":"She is **interested in** it. / It is **interesting**.","paragraphs":["Ask what causes the feeling. That cause commonly takes -ing: a confusing explanation, an interesting question. A person can also be the cause: a boring speaker is a speaker who bores others, not necessarily someone who feels bored."]},{"title":"Connect cause and effect","explanation":"Read who experiences the emotion and what causes it. The two forms belong to the same adjective family but have different roles.","examples":["A confusing instruction leaves a reader confused.","An exciting event leaves spectators excited.","A **tiring** journey left us **tired**."],"pattern":"The **excited** audience watched an **exciting** match.","paragraphs":["The pairing is lexical. Excited/exciting and tired/tiring are useful pairs, but not every word ending in -ed or -ing belongs to this system. The preposition following an adjective is also part of its pattern: interested in, worried about, pleased with."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"An -ing word can also be a verb form: the film is boring me contains a verb and object. The film is boring usually uses an adjective. Context and sentence structure reveal the role. Learn spelling changes with the adjective: worry → worried/worrying, not worryed.","examples":["The speaker was **boring**, so the audience felt **bored**.","I was **surprised** by the **surprising** result.","A **tiring** journey left us **tired**."]}],"recap":["I am **bored**. / The lesson is **boring**.","She is **interested in** it. / It is **interesting**.","The **excited** audience watched an **exciting** match."]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["adjective-order"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
